# Line endings that are load-bearing, declared instead of inherited from the
# cloner's `core.autocrlf`.
#
# A Windows batch file must be CRLF, and for lib/build-asaot.bat that is not
# cosmetic: cmd.exe's `^` line continuation swallows characters when the file has
# bare LF, and the script relies on it for the clang line. The toolchain pins it
# (test/unit/build.ts: "build-asaot.bat keeps CRLF line endings" asserts the file
# has \r\n and no bare \n).
#
# The file was written with CRLF and committed from a machine with
# `core.autocrlf=input`, which converts CRLF->LF on the way IN and never converts
# back on the way OUT. So the repository blob ended up LF while the author's
# working copy stayed CRLF -- the pin passed there and nobody noticed. A fresh
# clone (CI included, `actions/checkout` does not set autocrlf) wrote LF and the
# pin failed on every run.
#
# `text` normalizes to LF in the repository and `eol=crlf` restores CRLF on
# checkout, on every host, so the result no longer depends on who clones it.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# The shell siblings (lib/build-asaot.sh, examples/ane/build-ane.sh) are the same
# kind of file with the opposite requirement: a CRLF checkout would make sh treat
# the trailing \r as part of every command. Declared rather than left to the
# cloner's autocrlf, for the same reason as above.
*.sh text eol=lf
